1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,161
Three sigma is not a matter of taste. It

2
00:00:03,261 --> 00:00:06,260
is a bet, and we can price it exactly.

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:10,228
Level one told us which distribution every subgroup

4
00:00:10,328 --> 00:00:14,050
mean is drawn from, assuming nothing has changed.

5
00:00:14,150 --> 00:00:19,232
This curve is the null hypothesis. Not an assumption about the

6
00:00:19,332 --> 00:00:23,746
parts, but a claim about the process: it is unchanged.

7
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:27,499
Put limits at plus and minus three sigma of that curve,

8
00:00:27,599 --> 00:00:31,607
and you have drawn a boundary around ninety nine point seven

9
00:00:31,707 --> 00:00:35,373
three percent of everything the process should ever do.

10
00:00:35,466 --> 00:00:39,918
That leaves zero point two seven percent outside. Roughly one

11
00:00:40,018 --> 00:00:44,320
false alarm every three hundred and seventy subgroups. So a

12
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:47,902
point beyond the limit is a bet at three hundred

13
00:00:48,002 --> 00:00:51,782
and seventy to one that something really did change.

14
00:00:53,066 --> 00:00:55,691
A control chart is simply that same

15
00:00:55,791 --> 00:00:59,038
test, run again on every subgroup, forever.

16
00:00:59,133 --> 00:01:03,185
The limits are the boundary we just drew, turned on its side.

17
00:01:03,283 --> 00:01:08,032
Every point inside is the process agreeing with the null. This

18
00:01:08,132 --> 00:01:11,943
is what boring looks like, and boring is the goal.

19
00:01:12,033 --> 00:01:14,453
Then one point steps outside.

20
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:18,538
That point is not a bad part. It is evidence against

21
00:01:18,638 --> 00:01:23,806
the hypothesis that nothing changed. The correct response is not to

22
00:01:23,906 --> 00:01:27,266
scrap it. It is to go and find what changed.

